## The problem

Today a conjunction warning goes from ground radar, to an analyst, to an operator, and back up to the satellite. That loop takes 24 to 72 hours. Mega-constellations already do thousands of manoeuvres, so the loop does not scale.

## The approach

The ground still flags the risk. Then the satellite takes over: it looks at the object with its own sensors, finds the other satellite over an inter-satellite link, and the two agree on who moves.

The five steps are ground-to-orbit handover, onboard sensing, discovery, manoeuvre agreement, and the manoeuvre itself.

## What I'd do next

Test the agreement protocol under lossy links and with operators that do not fully trust each other.
